  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the presence or absence of a sure outcome and prefers lotteries without one. In this case, both lotteries lack a sure outcome, so the reason would either be indifferent or favor both equally. The individual, however, does not mention or reflect on whether an outcome is certain or not. Instead, the individual's justification is based on maximizing the potential gain ('eventuell mehr rauskommt'), which aligns more with a 'maximax' or aspiration-level heuristic—choosing the option with the highest possible outcome—rather than reasoning about certainty. There is no indication in the verbal report that the individual considered whether one of the lotteries had a sure outcome. The information used by the individual (magnitude of best outcome) differs from the information emphasized by the decision reason (presence of a sure outcome). Furthermore, the justification (choosing the higher potential gain) does not match the rule of the decision reason (prefer no sure outcome). Since the individual’s reasoning process does not align with the decision reason in either information considered or decision rule applied, it is unlikely that this reason was used. The absence of any mention of certainty or sure outcomes further supports this conclusion.",
